[
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev17-11",
    "question": "<p>(a) Use de Moivre&#x27;s theorem to show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin 5\\theta\\equiv 16 \\sin^{5} \\theta-20 \\sin^{3} \\theta+5 \\sin \\theta\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>(b) Hence find the five distinct solutions of the equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"16 x^{5}-20 x^{3}+5 x+\\frac{1}{2}=0\"></span> giving your answers to 3 decimal places where necessary. <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>(c) Use the identity given in (a) to find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\left( 4 \\sin^{5} \\theta-5 \\sin^{3} \\theta \\right) \\mathrm{d} \\theta\"></span> expressing your answer in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a \\sqrt{2}+b\"></span> , where a and b are rational numbers. <span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The imaginary part of the fifth power contains the odd sine powers. Replace each even power of cosine by a power of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1-\\sin^2\\theta\"></span> to obtain the identity in one variable. For the definite integral each frequency in a sine term contributes its own divisor on integration.</p>",
      "<p>(a) Equate imaginary parts of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)^5\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\cos5\\theta+i\\sin5\\theta\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Writing <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"s=\\sin\\theta,\\ c=\\cos\\theta\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"5c^4s-10c^2s^3+s^5\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Set <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"c^2=1-s^2\"></span> and collect like powers to obtain <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"16s^5-20s^3+5s\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Set <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=\\sin\\theta\"></span>; then <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin5\\theta=-1/2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>One complete choice is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"5\\theta=-\\pi/6+2k\\pi\"></span> for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k=0,1,2,3,4\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Taking the five distinct sine values gives -0.978, -0.500, -0.105, 0.669, 0.914; these exhaust the roots of the quintic. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) The integrand is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(\\sin5\\theta-5\\sin\\theta)/4\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Integrate to get <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"[-\\cos5\\theta/20+5\\cos\\theta/4]_0^{\\pi/4}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>The endpoint difference is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"13\\sqrt2/20-6/5\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 14 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev17-11-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2014 (R) FP2 · Q7, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev17-11-ms-02.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2014 (R) FP2 · Q7, extract 2\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev17-12",
    "question": "<div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"z=-2+\\left( 2\\sqrt{3} \\right) \\mathrm{i}\"></div><p>(a) Find the modulus and the argument of z.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p><p>Using de Moivre&#x27;s theorem,</p><p>(b) find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z^{6}\"></span> , simplifying your answer,</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(c) find the values of w such that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"w^{4}=z^{3}\"></span> , giving your answers in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a+\\mathrm{i} b\"></span> where a,b <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\in\\mathbb{R}.\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>A point in the second quadrant has principal argument <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\pi-\\pi/3\"></span>. Raising to the sixth power gives a whole number of turns, hence the result is real and positive. For the fourth roots of the positive real <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"64\"></span>, the arguments are separated by right angles.</p>",
      "<p>(a) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"|z|=\\sqrt{4+12}=4\"></span>, and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\arg z=\\pi-\\pi/3=2\\pi/3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[3]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z^6=4^6(\\cos4\\pi+i\\sin4\\pi)=4096\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z^3=64\"></span>; hence each fourth root has modulus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sqrt[4]{64}=2\\sqrt2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>The four arguments differ by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\pi/2\"></span>, giving <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"w=2\\sqrt2,\\ 2\\sqrt2 i,\\ -2\\sqrt2,\\ -2\\sqrt2 i\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 9 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev17-12-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2015 FP2 · Q2, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev17-13",
    "question": "<p>(a) Find the four roots of the equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z^{4}=8 \\left( \\sqrt{3}+i \\right)\"></span> in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z=r \\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{i} \\theta}\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>(b) Show these roots on an Argand diagram. <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Write the right side as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"16e^{i(\\pi/6+2k\\pi)}\"></span>. Its fourth roots satisfy <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4\\arg z=\\pi/6+2k\\pi\"></span>, so the small angle to the first point is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"7.5^\\circ\"></span>, while the spacing between successive points is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"90^\\circ\"></span>. This distinguishes orientation from spacing in the sketch.</p>",
      "<p>(a) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"8(\\sqrt3+i)\"></span> has modulus 16 and argument <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\pi/6\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>The fourth roots have modulus 2. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Their arguments are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\theta_k=\\pi/24+k\\pi/2\"></span>, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k=0,1,2,3\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z_k=2e^{i\\theta_k}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) The four points are equally spaced on the circle of radius 2, with the first at angle <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\pi/24\"></span> above the positive real axis. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<figure class=\"old-spec-diagram\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev17-13-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked diagram for REV17-13\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;max-width:100%;max-height:65mm\"></figure>",
      "<p>A check on all four answers at once is to raise <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2e^{i(\\pi/24+k\\pi/2)}\"></span> to the fourth power. It becomes <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"16e^{i(\\pi/6+2k\\pi)}=16(\\sqrt3/2+i/2)=8(\\sqrt3+i)\"></span>, independently of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\"></span>.</p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 7 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev17-13-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2016 FP2 · Q3, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev17-14",
    "question": "<p>(a) Use de Moivre&#x27;s theorem to show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin^{5}\\theta\\equiv a\\sin 5\\theta+b\\sin 3\\theta+c\\sin\\theta\"></span> where a,b and c are constants to be found. <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>(b) Hence show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\\sin^{5}\\theta\\mathrm{d}\\theta=\\frac{53}{480}\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>With <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z=e^{i\\theta}\"></span>, the two terms in <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z^j-z^{-j}\"></span> have equal real parts and opposite imaginary parts. Their difference is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2i\\sin j\\theta\"></span>. Pairing fifth powers this way avoids having to integrate <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin^5\\theta\"></span> by a separate substitution.</p>",
      "<p>(a) By de Moivre, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(z-z^{-1})^5=(2i\\sin\\theta)^5\"></span> with <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z=e^{i\\theta}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Expanding and grouping gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(z^5-z^{-5})-5(z^3-z^{-3})+10(z-z^{-1})\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Divide by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"32i\"></span> to obtain <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin^5\\theta=(\\sin5\\theta-5\\sin3\\theta+10\\sin\\theta)/16\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Integrate term by term: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"[-\\cos5\\theta/5+(5/3)\\cos3\\theta-10\\cos\\theta]_0^{\\pi/3}/16\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>The cosines at the upper limit are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/2,-1,1/2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Subtract the value at zero to obtain <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"[-1/10-5/3-5+1/5-5/3+10]/16=53/480\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 10 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev17-14-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2016 FP2 · Q5, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev17-15",
    "question": "<p>Solve the equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z^{3}+32+32 \\mathrm{i} \\sqrt{3}=0\"></span> giving your answers in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r \\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{i} \\theta}\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r&gt;0\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-\\pi&lt;\\theta\\leqslant\\pi\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Both coordinates of the right-hand side are negative, so its principal argument lies in quadrant three when measured anticlockwise, or is negative when expressed in the principal range. Add complete turns before dividing by three. Otherwise the cubic equation would appear to have only one root.</p>",
      "<p>Rearrange to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z^3=-32-32i\\sqrt3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Its modulus is 64, and the principal argument is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-2\\pi/3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"|z|=4\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3\\arg z=-2\\pi/3+2k\\pi\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>The three answers are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4e^{-8\\pi i/9},\\ 4e^{-2\\pi i/9},\\ 4e^{4\\pi i/9}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 6 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev17-15-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2017 FP2 · Q3, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev17-16",
    "question": "<p>(a) By writing <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{\\pi}{12}=\\frac{\\pi}{3}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\"></span> , show that</p><p>(i) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right)=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}\\right)\"></span></p><p>(ii) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right)=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}\\right)\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p><p>(b) Hence find the exact values of z for which</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"z^{4}=4\\left(\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{3}+\\mathrm{i}\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\"></div><p>Give your answers in the form z=a+ <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm{i} b\"></span> where a,b <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\in\\mathbb{R}\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The difference formulas use opposite signs: sine subtracts the mixed products, while cosine adds them for a difference of angles. In the roots calculation a quarter-turn multiplies a point by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"i\"></span>, sending <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(a,b)\"></span> to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(-b,a)\"></span>. Use this to obtain the remaining Cartesian roots from the first.</p>",
      "<p>(a) Use <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin(A-B)=\\sin A\\cos B-\\cos A\\sin B\"></span> with <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=\\pi/3,\\ B=\\pi/4\"></span>. Substitution gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(\\sqrt6-\\sqrt2)/4\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>For cosine use <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\cos A\\cos B+\\sin A\\sin B=(\\sqrt6+\\sqrt2)/4\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Each root has modulus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sqrt2\"></span> and arguments <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\pi/12+k\\pi/2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k=0\"></span> the root is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u+iv\"></span> with <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u=(\\sqrt3+1)/2,\\ v=(\\sqrt3-1)/2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Consecutive roots differ by a factor <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"i\"></span>, so all four are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u+iv,\\ -v+iu,\\ -u-iv,\\ v-iu\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 9 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev17-16-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2018 FP2 · Q3, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev17-16-ms-02.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2018 FP2 · Q3, extract 2\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  }
